Quality control: how the Council prevents bad decisions
This is the layer that makes the system more rigorous than a conventional brainstorming exercise.
The evidence test
Are the facts verified, current enough, and relevant? Is the conclusion being supported by evidence or by a plausible-sounding narrative?
The contradiction test
What is the strongest evidence against the current conclusion? Have opposing signals been investigated or merely explained away?
The alternative test
What other explanation or course of action could produce a better outcome?
The sensitivity test
Which assumption, missing fact, price change, injury, requirement, or other variable could materially change the recommendation?
The execution test
Can the recommended action actually be carried out with the available resources, time, permissions, budget, and information?
The outcome test
What evidence will establish whether the recommendation worked? What should be recorded for future comparisons?
The Council should reduce confidence when evidence is weak, not compensate for uncertainty by adding more specialist-sounding language.
